**Don't manage OS handles and Core Foundation objects by hand, rather use the
platform-specific scopers**, which release on destruction like any other smart
pointer.

| Platform                          | Type                              |
| --------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Windows `HANDLE`                  | `base::win::ScopedHandle`         |
| Core Foundation types (macOS/iOS) | `base::apple::ScopedCFTypeRef<T>` |

```cpp
// ❌ WRONG - leaks on every early return
HANDLE file = ::CreateFile(...);
if (!IsValid(file)) {
  return false;
}
::CloseHandle(file);

// ✅ CORRECT
base::win::ScopedHandle file(::CreateFile(...));
if (!file.IsValid()) {
  return false;
}
```

Note that the Core Foundation scoper lives in `base::apple::` — the old
`base::mac::ScopedCFTypeRef` spelling (still referenced by the upstream smart
pointer guidelines) no longer exists. See
[Chromium smart pointer guidelines](https://www.chromium.org/developers/smart-pointer-guidelines/).

wrong:
// ❌ WRONG - leaks on every early return
HANDLE file = ::CreateFile(...);
if (!IsValid(file)) {
  return false;
}
::CloseHandle(file);

right:
// ✅ CORRECT
base::win::ScopedHandle file(::CreateFile(...));
if (!file.IsValid()) {
  return false;
}

body:
**Always include the headers for types you directly use.** Don't rely on
transitive includes from other headers - they can change at any time and break
your code.

```cpp
// ❌ WRONG - relying on transitive includes
#include "base/memory/ref_counted.h"
// Uses std::string but doesn't include <string>

// ✅ CORRECT - include what you use
#include <string>
#include "base/memory/ref_counted.h"
```

Also remove includes you don't actually use. Double-check all includes in new
files.

**For type aliases:** include the header that declares the type alias, not the
headers for the underlying types. Same principle as class inheritance — if class
B inherits from A, include B's header, not A's.

**Exception — GN-private headers:** don't include a header that is private to
its GN target (not in the target's `public` list, and your target isn't on its
`friend`/`visibility` allowlist) — the GN header check will fail. Include the
header the owner exports for that purpose instead, often a `*_test_util.h` or
`mock_*.h` from a test-support target. Check the owning target in `BUILD.gn`
first, and **NEVER flag the missing private include in review.**

```cpp
// ❌ WRONG - net/dns/host_resolver.h is private to //net/dns:host_resolver
#include "net/dns/host_resolver.h"

// ✅ CORRECT - exported by the test header upstream intends for this
#include "net/dns/mock_host_resolver.h"
```

wrong:
// ❌ WRONG - relying on transitive includes
#include "base/memory/ref_counted.h"
// Uses std::string but doesn't include <string>

right:
// ✅ CORRECT - include what you use
#include <string>
#include "base/memory/ref_counted.h"

